{
  "requirement": "Every {term}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.criterion` type implements\n`trust_but_verify::graph::Criterion<D>` for its\n{term}`requirements.sdk.application_domain`.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Criterion<D: Domain>: Send + Sized {\n    type Ok: Serialize + DeserializeOwned + Send;\n\n    fn source_node_id(&self) -> NodeId<D>;\n    fn criterion_path(&self) -> CriterionPath;\n    fn version(&self) -> CriterionVersion;\n    fn execute(\n        self,\n        ctx: &mut ValidationContext<'_, D>,\n    ) -> impl Future<Output = Result<Self::Ok, ValidationError<D::Error>>> + Send;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judge the tests adequate for `requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.criterion_trait`. The requirement is a structural contract. Every criterion type implements `Criterion<D>` for its domain, with the stated associated type and four methods, and it is `Send + Sized`. Rust enforces that contract at compile time, so tests check it by implementing and using the trait. I did not read the implementation source. I relied on the supplied test text and on the trait and `Review` impl sites that `references` listed.\n\n- **Implementations compile against the trait.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[criteria_keep_typed_answers_and_persist_only_when_requested]` uses a `Review` criterion in a test application domain. It runs `validation::verify`, `store_evidence` and `load_evidence`, which exercise `execute`, `source_node_id`, `criterion_path` and `version`. It asserts on the `Ok` output through serialize and deserialize round trips, including a `Vec<String>` answer and a unit answer.\n- **Send bounds.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[validation_and_answer_need_send_without_sync]` runs a criterion through a `send(...)` helper. It uses a `Cell<u32>` answer, which is `Send` but not `Sync`. This matches `Ok: Serialize + DeserializeOwned + Send` and the `Send` future returned by `execute`.\n- **A second domain.** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#fn[deterministic_validation_uses_required_storage_without_an_agent]` and `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[caller_reuses_negative_answers_and_compares_revisions_explicitly]` use another `Memory` domain and its `Review` criteria. The first verifies and stores evidence and asserts the output and the recorded dependency. The second asserts version compatibility between revisions.\n- **A real SDK criterion.** `src/authoring/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[domain_persists_and_reuses_a_negative_validation_through_the_sdk]` calls `source_node_id()`, `criterion_path()` and `version()` directly on the SDK `Review` criterion. It also checks that its answer type round-trips through storage.\n- **Public export.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` checks that the public API matches the documented inventory, which covers `trust_but_verify::graph::Criterion` being exported at that path.\n\nThe per-member obligations (answer, `source_node_id`, version, `execute`) have their own refining requirements, so I did not assess them here. There are no `{fixme}` acknowledgments and I found no gap.",
  "references": "b8aba858074b87917d741e6bdb62f922585d791a0b517182e03e288af06cf211",
